Multi-query test: one query per edge worker, each scoring against that worker's OWN centroid on B
Date: 2026-11-11, local Mac (Docker Desktop, Apple Silicon), pinned nes-worker digest sha256:314e7861...
Data: committed data/worker_01..10.csv (20 Newsgroups, 32-dim, 200 rows each).
Centroid of worker i = normalised mean of worker i's own 200 rows.

VERDICT: Yes. One topology file with a list of 10 queries deploys 10 independent queries; each spans
only its own edge worker and B, and all 10 deliver complete, exact output in ~3 s.

Design (per worker i):  A_i [File source -> network]  -->  B [SCORE vs centroid_i -> results_ii.csv]
  one logical source per worker (EMB_i), one sink per worker (RESULTS_i), max_operators: 0 on A_i.
  (Not used: one shared source + WHERE WORKER_ID = i, which would send every row to every query.)

Result     | Queries deployed | Rows                    | Embeddings vs. input | SCORE vs. Python
2 workers  | 2 ids            | 200/200 each            | identical            | max diff 7.1e-07
10 workers | 10 ids           | 200/200 each, 2000 total| identical            | max diff 7.1e-07
No row ended up in another worker's file.

Placement (nes-cli dump, 10 workers): 20 plans = 10 on the A-workers (source -> network only) and
10 on worker-b; all 10 SCORE projections run on worker-b.
`status` works for each query id; all ended Stopped after their File source finished.

Does "own centroid" mean something? Mean score of a worker's rows against its own centroid is
+0.27 to +0.49; against the other 9 workers' centroids it is -0.06 to -0.01. So B can tell whether a
row is typical for the node that sent it (basis for per-node anomaly detection).

Errors/warnings: only the known benign ones (startup "Channel ... rejected", teardown
validateFinalState / SequenceShredder). Nothing else.
Memory (docker stats, idle after the run): worker-b 240 MiB with 10 queries (vs ~235 MiB with one),
A-workers ~203 MiB.
Gotcha (test scripting, not NebulaStream): in zsh an unmatched glob like `rm -f output/*.csv` aborts
the command, so a `&&` chain after it never ran. Use `setopt nullglob` or `find`.
